@0    = ~Zzz...~
@1    = ~Hey. Hey, Cernd. Are you asleep? Can you hear me? You got any food you could throw over?~ [FHF72]
@2    = ~Wha-what? Oh. Must've dozed off, there. Damn rooms with no windows...~
@3    = ~I can hear you all too well, my friend. I fear these walls are crumbling under the weight of ages, and do not afford much privacy.~
@4    = ~Don't encourage him, druid. Blacknall, shut up.~
@5    = ~Oh, at least let me talk to my cellmate. Come on, Cooper, I'm so damn bored in here. How long have I got left?~
@6    = ~Maybe you should've thought twice before starting that fight, shouldn't you?~
@7    = ~You punched first! Soon as they let me out of here, I'm going to--~
@8    = ~Yeah, yeah, you'll make me sorry. Where have I heard that before? Now, shut up and let me think.~
@9    = ~Why is this man in prison?~
@10   = ~What is going on, here?~
@11   = ~That's a nasty black eye you've got, there.~
@12   = ~Nothing, I'll just be off, now.~
@13   = ~Hey. Hey, Cooper. Are you asleep? Can you hear me? You got any food you could throw in here?~ [FHF73]
@14   = ~Wha-what? Oh. Must've dozed off. Shut up, Blacknall.~
@15   = ~Oh, at least a biscuit or something to tide me over. Come on, Cooper, I'm so damn bored in here. How long have I got left?~
@16   = ~Yeah, yeah, you'll make me sorry. Where have I heard that before?~
@17   = ~Unfairness and slander, and--~
@18   = ~Shut up, Blacknall. Faren Blacknall's here 'cause he got drunk and started a fight over in Vyatri's pub again. Lord Coprith decided he needed to cool off for a bit in here. Best place for him, I say.~
@19   = ~That is completely... well, it's mostly true. Except for the 'best place' bit.~
@20   = ~And I'm here 'cause... I was involved in the fight, let's say. So they thought it would be good discipline to make me watch him and make sure he didn't throw up or something.~
@21   = ~Do you know when he's due to be let out?~
@22   = ~So would he be able to be let out early?~
@23   = ~Well, I'll leave you to it, then.~
@24   = ~He squealed like a girl when I got him, too.~
@25   = ~Shut up, Blacknall. Listen, <PRO_SIRMAAM>, Faren Blacknall's here 'cause he got drunk and started a fight over in Vyatri's pub again. Lord Coprith decided he needed to cool off for a bit in here. Best place for him, I say.~
@26   = ~Don't go, you're way more interesting than Cooper and his lot!~
@27   = ~I told you, keep your mouth shut! Now, what was it Matheson said about the tactics of scaling buildings...?~
@28   = ~We're letting him out tomorrow, all going well - and if he doesn't keep giving me lip.~
@29   = ~'Course, if someone was to pay his bail, that'd be a different matter - but who'd do that here? We all know he's not worth trusting.~
@30   = ~What if I were to pay his bail for him?~
@31   = ~I understand. Well, I'll be going, now.~
@32   = ~Huh? Well, it's up to you if you want to waste your money. Talk to Lord Coprith if you want to sort that out.~
@33   = ~Yeah? What do you want? I'm busy, here. Very busy.~
@34   = ~Well, you're allowed out now. Get out of here, and good riddance. Here's your things.~
@35   = ~Credit to the guards, you are. Now, let's see about getting out of here...~
@36   = ~Ah, it's you again. <CHARNAME>, isn't it? I heard the guards talking about you - something about the animal attacks? Good to see you properly, at last.~
@37   = ~And another thing, you wouldn't have anything to do with me being let out early, would you?~
@38   = ~Actually, I've sorted out that problem.~
@39   = ~Well, when I found out about the problems here I thought I could help out.~
@40   = ~I paid your bail. You don't mind, do you?~
@41   = ~No, not at all. They must have just wanted to let you go early.~
@42   = ~What are your plans, now you're free?~
@43   = ~Well, I'm glad you're free now, but I really must be going.~
@44   = ~Really? Well, congratulations - you must be able to handle yourself pretty well. I wish I'd been there to see it, but as you see, I've been a bit occupied.~
@45   = ~That's very noble of you. Hope it goes well.~
@46   = ~Don't mind? You've got my heartfelt thanks, <CHARNAME>. It's a hell of a debt, but I don't mind owing it to you. You look like a reasonable enough <PRO_MANWOMAN>.~
@47   = ~How about you travel with me? Think of it as repaying that debt.~
@48   = ~Just remember that you can pay me back someday.~
@49   = ~Thank you! So what are your plans, now you're out?~
@50   = ~Sure they did...because that's such a likely thing for them to do. Well, no matter. Thanks for coming back, anyway. It's good to see a new face.~
@51   = ~It's no problem. What do you plan to do, now you're out?~
@52   = ~I was actually wondering if you could join me. You look like you'd be useful.~
@53   = ~Sure thing. I'll see you around, Faren.~
@54   = ~I'll head over to Vyatri's, make sure he's not too angry at me - I expect he's got that table fixed by now, anyway. Beyond that, who knows? It's been a while since I've had much of anything on my schedule.~
@55   = ~If you think you'll be at a loose end, why not join with me?~
@56   = ~Sounds thrilling. See you around, Faren.~
@57   = ~Perhaps you should try to stay out of trouble, from now on.~
@58   = ~Right then. Maybe we'll see more of each other - look for me in Vyatri's if you feel like catching up.~
@59   = ~Right, well, I'm headed over to Vyatri's. Should be able to do enough apologising that he'll let me hang around there a bit.~
@60   = ~Course I will. Come see me at Vyatri's sometime, and we can settle the debt. I wonder if he's got that table fixed yet...~
@61   = ~It's not every day someone waltzes into town and wants me by their side. What exactly do you need me for?~
@62   = ~You seem like a good fighter. You can't get enough protection with the things I do.~
@63   = ~Maybe I'd like to have you around. You look like you know how to have fun.~
@64   = ~Do you have anything else that you'd prefer to do?~
@65   = ~From the look of you, I don't think you like to stay out of trouble - so I think you'd be able to understand why I don't either!~
@66   = ~Maybe this isn't the time to tell you that I lost that fight. I was outnumbered, though, and you should have seen the other guys afterwards!~
@67   = ~Fun? Some of that would be good. I wouldn't mind stretching my legs, either...~
@68   = ~You got me there. I like Trademeet well enough, but not all of Trademeet likes me. It'll probably do me good to hit the road again.~
@69   = ~There's...er, something you should probably know - I haven't had much good luck with adventuring groups. In fact, the last group I was with...most of them kind of died.~
@70   = ~We were over east, and heard a village was having problems with strange deaths. We headed over to have a look and next thing we knew, there was a huge mass of dark things which decided my friends would make a tasty dinner.~
@71   = ~That sounds like the Shade Lord in the Umar Hills. I killed it.~
@72   = ~Ah, yes. I've agreed to investigate that matter for the Mayor of Imnesvale.~
@73   = ~That sounds familiar. I spoke to a boy from the east about a problem in his village.~
@74   = ~Where did you say this place was? It sounds like it could be interesting.~
@75   = ~I hardly think you having bad luck is a reason to leave you behind. Come, join me.~
@76   = ~On second thought, that does sound like you don't have the best judgement. I'll see you around.~
@77   = ~You...killed that thing? By the gods! Well, I'll be happy to join you, then! If you can kill that, I'd like to see anything else get a bite out of you!~
@78   = ~I'm trying to rescue someone from a terrible fate, and I need all the help I can get.~
@79   = ~I'm hunting a mage at the moment, and you look like you could help.~
@80   = ~Then let's go.~
@81   = ~Oh. Well. That puts a new spin on things. Still, I'd like to get out of here.~
@82   = ~It's more 'terrifying' than 'interesting', but I can show you if you want.~
@83   = ~I like your attitude, <CHARNAME>. Good to know you'll give me a chance.~
@84   = ~Terrible, huh? Well, I'd be happy to help out. Sounds interesting, anyway, which is more than I've got here.~
@85   = ~I'm not the best tracker, but I can help out if you want. Sounds interesting, anyway, which is more than I've got here.~
@86   = ~Ah, <CHARNAME>, good to see you again. What's up?~
@87   = ~I was wondering if you wanted to join with me, Faren.~
@88   = ~Can you pay me back the price of your bail?~
@89   = ~Nothing much. I just wanted to see how you were doing.~
@90   = ~I was just swinging by. You haven't been getting into any more fights, have you?~
@91   = ~Things are getting decidedly...complicated, in my life.~
@92   = ~It's not every day someone like you waltzes into town and creates such a ruckus. You look like you're handling yourself pretty well, though, so what do you need me for?~
@93   = ~Sure. Here you go - thanks again for getting me out.~
@94   = ~If you had the money, why didn't you pay them in the first place?~
@95   = ~It was my pleasure, don't worry about it.~
@96   = ~Now that's settled, I was wondering if you wanted to join up with me. You look like you're at a loose end.~
@97   = ~Aha, I knew you paid it! I don't know why you didn't want to say before, but here you go - thanks for getting me out, even if you didn't want to mention it.~
@98   = ~You're welcome - it was a pleasure.~
@99   = ~Now that's settled, I was wondering if you wanted to join up with me? You look like you're at a loose end.~
@100  = ~Same old things. I'd ask Vyatri to liven things up, but he'd probably take it the wrong way.~
@101  = ~If you're at a loose end, how about you join me?~
@102  = ~You look bored. Why don't you get out and do something, instead of sitting around here?~
@103  = ~Well, keep out of trouble. I'd better be going.~
@104  = ~No, it's just the same old things. I'd ask Vyatri to liven things up, but he'd probably take it the wrong way.~
@105  = ~You need a hand or something, just ask. I don't normally offer help after a sob story, but if you were to ask...~
@106  = ~Well then, can you join me? I think you'd be a great help.~
@107  = ~No, I don't need your help right now. I was just saying what was up.~
@108  = ~I do have the money, but if it was a choice between hanging around for a few days, and paying? Sitting there didn't really bother me. It was nice of you to worry enough to get me out, though.~
@109  = ~Fair enough. Take care, Faren - I'd best get going, now.~
@110  = ~Good to hear, good to hear. Now, surely you have better things to do than hang around here?~
@111  = ~Yes, I should be going.~
@112  = ~I was actually wondering if you'd like to join up with me. You look like you're at a loose end.~
@113  = ~I could, but it's never good to hit the road alone.~
@114  = ~Then join with me. I think you'd make a great addition to the group.~
@115  = ~Too true. Well, I should be going.~
@116  = ~It was good to see you again. Take care.~
@117  = ~I spoke to Faren, the other prisoner. Why do you keep him contained?~
@118  = ~Is there any way you can release Faren sooner?~
@119  = ~I will do so soon. Farewell.~
@120  = ~I will do my best to help him. Farewell.~
@121  = ~But I need to know when Faren will be released!~
@122  = ~I thank you for your well-wishes. Farewell.~
@123  = ~Thank you for the advice. Farewell.~
@124  = ~I suppose it was inevitable that curiosity would draw you in. He is not dangerous as such, but he has been a nuisance lately, and I decided it would be best to have him confined for a short while.~
@125  = ~He is not unpleasant, but he has a quick temper. I am in no hurry to have him loose amongst my men again.~
@126  = ~How long will he be confined in the prison for?~
@127  = ~You speak fairly. I shall bow to your judgement, then, and will wait for his release.~
@128  = ~Very well. I hope to speak with you soon.~
@129  = ~I suppose it was inevitable that curiosity would draw you in. Faren Blacknall is not dangerous as such, but he has been a nuisance lately, and I decided it would be best to have him confined.~
@130  = ~I am in no hurry to release him, but were his bail to be paid he would go free.~
@131  = ~How much would it cost to pay his bail?~
@132  = ~If I were to pay the bail, would he be allowed out early?~
@133  = ~Never mind, then. I will leave you be.~
@134  = ~You are unwelcome here nonetheless. Get out of my sight.~
@135  = ~Actually, his time is up. You may go and tell him the good news, if you wish - I am rather occupied at the moment, as you see.~
@136  = ~He is still under our guard at present, and will be until tomorrow.~
@137  = ~Is there any way you can release him sooner?~
@138  = ~Never mind, then. I will be on my way.~
@139  = ~If his bail were to be paid, he would be released. That is how things work, here.~
@140  = ~It is 250 gp. It may seem steep, but this is not the first time, and I am hoping to discourage his behaviour.~
@141  = ~No, your reputation precedes you. I do not think it wise to consider your word a suitable guarantee for Faren's good behaviour.~
@142  = ~Please reconsider - I believe my reputation is undeserved.~
@143  = ~Fine, then. I'll leave you to it.~
@144  = ~You would do this for a man you barely know? Well, that is a surprising thing indeed, in these times.~
@145  = ~That is the sort of generous behaviour I have heard about from others - it seems that the rumours were correct after all.~
@146  = ~You would do this for a man you barely know? That is surprising indeed, in these times. Do you have the money to give?~
@147  = ~Yes, I am willing to do it. Here's the money.~
@148  = ~I am afraid that I do not have enough.~
@149  = ~No, that's too expensive for me.~
@150  = ~That is the sort of generous behaviour I have heard about from others, <CHARNAME>. Very well - do you have the money?~
@151  = ~Yes, I have it and am willing to do it. Here's the money.~
@152  = ~There is a fee of 250 gp as assurance that Faren will behave himself in the future. Are you prepared to give that money?~
@153  = ~Yes, I am willing to do that. Here's the money.~
@154  = ~I am afraid that I do not have enough money.~
@155  = ~Your demeanour does not convince me that this is a wise course to take. No, if he wishes to have his bail paid, then he can arrange for it himself.~
@156  = ~As you wish. You are very generous, though I do not quite understand your motive for doing such a thing.~
@157  = ~You may go and tell him the good news - do not expect a great deal of gratitude, however. He is an unpredictable sort.~
@158  = ~Then you will have to wait until he is allowed out. If you wish to speak with him, you can do so then.~
